cartridge "SWOOP/AHOY! 1986/2026"

' SWOOP
' A ColecoVision game based on "Swoop", Ahoy! June 1986 (Commodore 64
' machine language at $C000, started by a two-line BASIC boot). The disk
' and the program name no author; the magazine's byline could not be
' checked, so the credit is "Ahoy! June 1986". Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/swoop.dis, and timed in jsc64, orig/c64time-swoop.mjs),
' the game is built for the ColecoVision: a night sky over a hill village,
' a lantern-cannon on wheels, six bats with three wing beats, embers, a
' title tune and sound effects.
'
' The game: six bats flutter about the night sky, each in a straight line
' that it changes at random (or when it meets the edge). Your cannon rolls
' along the hill and fires one shot at a time straight up. A bat right
' above the cannon may let an ember fall. An ember, or a bat, that touches
' the cannon costs a life. Clear all six for the next level: the bats fly
' faster and each is worth 10 more points.
'
' From the original (orig/swoop.dis), in the C64's sprite pixels: the
' bats' steps 1, -1, 2, -2, 3, -3, 1, -2 (picked at random for X and Y
' apart), a new heading when a step would leave X 28-319 or Y 52-207 and
' one time in 64 anyway; their speed from the main loop's delay
' (C585 x 256 empty loops, C585 = 21 - level, down to 8 at level 13),
' measured in jsc64 and worked out for a 60 Hz NTSC C64 into passes per
' frame (RATE); the cannon's 2 pixels a frame between X 28 and 318; the
' shot one 8-pixel row a frame for 19 rows, one at a time; an ember only
' when no ember is falling and the first bat (from the sixth down) whose
' X equals the cannon's is alive and high enough; the ember's 4 pixels a
' pass; 10 x level points a bat; a life every 1000 points (at most 9);
' 3 lives; a death or a cleared sky waits 100 frames; each new wave the
' cannon blinks 7 times 10 frames apart and the bats come down from the
' top for 55 frames; a death sets the cannon back to X 176 and brings all
' six bats back; the score and the high score (six digits).
'
' Liberties: the field is scaled (X by 5/7, Y by 7/8) while every rule
' stays in the C64's pixels; collisions are box tests on the drawn shapes
' (the C64 used its collision registers); the shot is a smooth sprite (the
' C64 drew it with line characters); the bats turn back at Y 186 (the C64
' let them fly down into the cannon) and only drop embers from above Y
' 120 (the C64: 170), both so that a human player can dodge (a bot that
' reacts 15 frames late reached level 9 on average; with the C64's numbers
' it died on level 2); the bats come down to three staggered heights (the
' C64 lined all six up at Y 55); the fire button is read every frame (the
' C64 read it once a loop pass); the ember glows steadily and the cannon's
' blast does not flash (the C64 changed their colours every pass, faster
' than 3 a second); a bat that is hit leaves a puff for a moment; the
' stick's diagonals count as left or right only if one is pressed; GAME
' OVER, then the title (the C64 said PRESS ANY KEY); the pause on SHIFT is
' left out.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the cannon left and right,
' either button fires. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

